<BaseLayout title="Methodology">
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: "About", href: "/about/" }, { label: "Methodology" }]} />
  <h1>Methodology</h1>
  <p class="page-intro">How the project finds, checks, explains, and updates U.S. genetics and genomics law and policy records.</p>

  <section class="reading-width">
    <h2>How to read a record</h2>
    <p>Start with the status and review date. Then read what the law or policy requires or prohibits, followed by its important limits and exceptions. Use the linked primary source to confirm the current official text.</p>

    <h2>Project scope</h2>
    <p>The project tracks U.S. federal, state, district, and territorial law and policy concerning genetic information, genomic data, biological samples, genetic testing, and related uses. Coverage is not comprehensive and expands through source review.</p>

    <h2 id="inclusion-criteria">Inclusion criteria</h2>
    <p>A record must identify the relevant government, official source, type of record, legal or policy status, and connection to a defined genetics or genomics topic. Candidate records remain outside the verified dataset until a human reviewer checks the primary source.</p>

    <h2>Exclusion criteria</h2>
    <p>The project excludes unsupported claims, duplicate records, secondary summaries presented as legal authority, and candidate changes that have not completed review. General privacy or health laws may be excluded until a source documents how they apply to genetic information.</p>

    <h2>Source hierarchy</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Enacted statutory text</li>
      <li>Legislature or government bill page</li>
      <li>Official regulation</li>
      <li>Official agency guidance</li>
      <li>Court opinion</li>
      <li>Official government archive</li>
      <li>Credible legal or policy secondary source</li>
      <li>News reporting when necessary for context</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="nhgri-provenance">NHGRI provenance</h2>
    <p>The complete NHGRI Genome Statute and Legislation Database is included in the combined Policy Records directory. Its original state, primary link, topic, status, and summary are preserved. Local topic mappings support search and do not establish legal equivalence.</p>
    <p>These entries identify NHGRI as their source and display the retrieval timestamp. NHGRI’s source page was last updated February 8, 2024. Current legal decisions should always use the linked primary source.</p>

    <h2>Status vocabulary</h2>
    <p>All records use the NHGRI status vocabulary: Approved, Died, Enacted, Passed House, Passed Senate, Statute, Pending, and Introduced. Record type remains separate so statutes, regulations, policies, and legislation retain their substantive form.</p>

    <h2>Research and review process</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Locate an official government source.</li>
      <li>Determine the jurisdiction, record type, status, dates, and scope.</li>
      <li>Draft a factual summary separately from plain-language interpretation.</li>
      <li>Assign controlled topic tags and document uncertainty.</li>
      <li>Run schema and controlled-vocabulary validation.</li>
      <li>Complete human source and summary review before publication.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Evidence informing this method</h2>
    <p>A targeted PubMed Central search conducted through Paperclip on September 2, 2026, informed selected parts of this methodology. The search focused on legal epidemiology, transparent search reporting, and plain-language summaries. It was used to refine the project’s method and writing, not to identify the legal records.</p>
    <p>Published legal epidemiology methods describe policy surveillance as the systematic tracking and comparison of policies.<a class="citation-reference" href="#reference-1" aria-label="Reference 1">[1]</a> Guidance on reporting literature searches supports recording information sources, search details, and execution dates.<a class="citation-reference" href="#reference-2" aria-label="Reference 2">[2]</a> Work on public-facing research summaries supports consistent structure, clear limitations, and a careful balance between plain language and precision.<a class="citation-reference" href="#reference-3" aria-label="Reference 3">[3]</a></p>
    <p>This was a focused background search, not a systematic or scoping review. The project does not claim adherence to PRISMA-S or another evidence-synthesis reporting guideline.</p>

    <h2>Uncertainty and absence</h2>
    <p>“No specific law identified” describes a dataset finding, not a definitive conclusion that no law exists. Legal coverage can depend on definitions, exceptions, effective dates, preemption, enforcement, and the facts of a particular situation.</p>

    <h2>Corrections and versioning</h2>
    <p>Corrections create a record-history entry and increment the record version when the substantive public record changes. GitHub releases provide stable snapshot identifiers and checksum-verified data files.</p>

    <h2>Automation boundary</h2>
    <p>A weekly monitor compares the checked-in NHGRI snapshot with a newly retrieved candidate. Structured changes are presented in a draft pull request for source and data review. Retrieval timestamps and cosmetic page changes do not create a new snapshot when the policy records are unchanged.</p>
    <p>Automated systems must not rewrite legal summaries, merge candidate data, or publish legal conclusions without human review.</p>

